        ["Inspection of chimney structure and condition assessment","Scaffolding or lift setup for safe elevated work","Top-down demolition of masonry and flue system","Structural patching of roof and interior wall openings","Debris removal and site cleanup"],
        [{question:"Is chimney removal dangerous?",answer:"Chimney removal carries risks including falling masonry and roof damage. Our Georgia crews use proper scaffolding, protective equipment, and controlled demolition techniques to ensure safe removal."},{question:"What happens to the roof after chimney removal?",answer:"We professionally patch and weatherproof the roof opening after chimney removal, matching existing roofing materials where possible. We recommend a roofer complete final repairs for warranty purposes."},{question:"Do I need a permit to remove a chimney in Georgia?",answer:"Permit requirements vary by Georgia county. Many counties require a structural permit for chimney removal. We verify requirements with your local building department before starting."},{question:"Can you remove just the chimney above the roofline?",answer:"Yes, we offer partial chimney removal above the roofline if you want to keep the interior fireplace. This is common in Georgia historic homes where the interior is still functional."}],
        "Chimney removal is one of the most requested demolition services in Georgia, particularly in older neighborhoods of Atlanta, Savannah, Augusta, and Macon where brick chimneys are common in homes built before 1980. Whether you're dealing with a deteriorating chimney that poses a structural risk, removing an unused fireplace to reclaim interior space, or demolishing a chimney as part of a larger renovation, Georgia Demolition & Removal has the equipment and expertise to handle the job safely. Our crews are experienced in removing brick, stone, and block chimneys from single-story ranch homes to three-story historic structures across Georgia.",
        "Chimney removal requires a top-down approach to maintain structural integrity throughout the process. Our Georgia crews begin by inspecting the chimney for asbestos-containing materials, particularly in homes built before 1980. If asbestos is detected, we coordinate licensed abatement before proceeding. Demolition uses hand tools, mechanical chippers, and mini-excavators for foundation-level work. All brick and masonry is separated for recycling at Georgia concrete recycling facilities when material quality allows.",
        "In historic Georgia neighborhoods like Inman Park and Midtown Atlanta, Ardsley Park in Savannah, and Summerville in Augusta, chimney removal may be subject to Historic Preservation Commission review. Our team is familiar with local historic district regulations across Georgia. Additionally, many older Georgia homes have chimneys that served oil or gas appliances and may have soot or oil residue requiring disposal per Georgia EPD guidelines. We handle all regulatory compliance as part of our standard chimney removal service."
