        "Professional garage demolition in Georgia. Attached and detached garage teardown, debris removal, and site clearing. Free estimates statewide.",
        "Garage Demolition Services in Georgia",
        ["Attached and detached garage demolition","Utility disconnection before demolition","Slab removal or leave-in-place options","Matching exterior repair for attached garage walls","Complete debris removal and site cleanup"],
        ["Utility disconnection (electric, gas, water) coordination","Permit acquisition with county building department","Roof and wall structure demolition","Slab breaking and removal if required","Site cleanup, grading, and erosion control"],
        [{question:"Can you demolish an attached garage without damaging my home?",answer:"Yes. Attached garage demolition is our specialty. We use precise cutting and structural assessment to safely remove the garage while protecting the shared wall and roof connection with your home."},{question:"Do I need a permit to demolish a garage in Georgia?",answer:"Yes, most Georgia counties require a demolition permit for garage teardown. We handle the permit process and utility disconnect notifications for you."},{question:"Can you convert a garage space rather than demolish it?",answer:"While we specialize in demolition, we can remove specific elements like walls or slabs to support a garage conversion. Consult with a general contractor for the conversion portion of the work."},{question:"How much does garage demolition cost in Georgia?",answer:"Detached garage demolition typically costs $2,500–$8,000 in Georgia. Attached garages may cost more due to the structural complexity of shared wall separation and repair."}],
        "Garage demolition is one of the most common requests Georgia homeowners make to our team. Aging detached garages that have become structurally unsound, attached garages being removed for additions or remodels, and carports needing replacement are all common scenarios across Georgia. The state's diverse housing stock includes everything from brick detached garages in Augusta's Summerville neighborhood to aluminum-framed carports in south Georgia farm communities to modern two-car attached garages in Alpharetta and Johns Creek subdivisions.",
        "Garage demolition follows a systematic sequence. We begin with utility disconnections, working with local Georgia power companies like Georgia Power, GRMC, and Sawnee EMC to safely disconnect electrical service. Gas disconnection is coordinated with Atlanta Gas Light or other Georgia natural gas providers. The demolition sequence proceeds from the roof down—removing roofing, then roof structure, then wall framing or masonry, then the slab if required. For attached garages, we use hand tools and precise cutting to separate the garage from the main structure, then make weatherproofing repairs to the exposed home wall.",
        "Georgia's HOA-heavy suburban communities in Cherokee, Forsyth, Gwinnett, and other metro Atlanta counties often have architectural review requirements for garage demolition projects. Some HOAs prohibit removal of garages that were part of the original construction design. Always review your HOA covenants before scheduling garage demolition. In terms of county permitting, Cobb County, Fulton County, and DeKalb County have detailed permit requirements including erosion control plans for garage demolition."
